INSERT INTO theory_content (chapter_slug, subject_id, concepts_json) VALUES ('telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka', 'telangana-history-en', '') ON CONFLICT(chapter_slug) DO UPDATE SET concepts_json = '';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || '[{"partNumber": 1, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p1", "partName": "Vakatakas — Overview and Territory", "bodyMd": "The Vakatakas hold a special place among the dynasties that ruled the Deccan, spanning the middle of the third century to the middle of the sixth century CE. They were the most important dynasty influencing Deccan culture in this period.\n\n*   After the fall of the Satavahana empire, the **Abhiras** declared independence near Nasik in Maharashtra, while the **Vakatakas** declared independence in **Eastern Berar**.\n*   The Vakatakas had earlier been feudatories under the Satavahanas before asserting independence.\n*   Vakatakas ruled for roughly three centuries, from **250 CE to 560 CE** (also given elsewhere as up to 650 CE), providing political stability across the Deccan and Central India.\n*   They ensured law and order and, being liberal in religious matters, patronized education, literature, and the arts.\n\n**Matrimonial Alliances**\n\n*   Guptas and Nagas in the north\n*   Vishnukundus in the eastern Deccan\n*   Kadambas in the western Deccan\n\n**Territorial Extent**\n\n*   From the Arabian Sea in the west to Chhattisgarh in the east\n*   From Malwa, Gujarat, Bundelkhand and Bagelkhand in the north to the banks of the Tungabhadra River in the south\n*   The Nanded and Aurangabad districts of Maharashtra, and parts of Telangana such as **Adilabad**, were ruled by the **Vatsagulma branch** of the Vakatakas\n*   Vidarbha and Bagelkhand areas formed the key political center of the Vakatakas\n\n**Capitals** Early Vakataka inscriptions mention ''Purika Pattanam'' and ''Chanaka'' as their earliest capitals. Later, ''Pravarapura'' (Paunar), near Wardha, became their capital.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 2, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p2", "partName": "Historical Sources", "bodyMd": "### Inscriptions\n\nAmong the inscriptional sources of Vakataka history, only a few are stone slab inscriptions — the rest are inscribed on copper plates.\n\n*   Among the copper plate inscriptions, only a few belong to the **Vatsagulma (Basim)** branch; the majority belong to the **Nandivardhana–Pravarapura** branch.\n*   Vakataka inscriptions are written in **Sanskrit** language and **Brahmi** script.\n\n**Inscriptions of the Nandivardhana–Pravarapura Branch Rulers**\n\n| Inscription | Details |\n|---|---|\n| Mandhal copper plate | Issued by Rudrasena in his fifth regnal year; indicates his affection for Vaishnavism |\n| Indore, Yavatmal, Masod, Mandhal | Inscriptions of Pravarasena II. Masod inscriptions were issued while granting land to 19 Brahmins; Yavatmal inscriptions record the re-allotment of already-donated land |\n| Mandthal, Maharjari | Inscriptions of Prithvisena II |\n| Miregaon | Issued by Prabhavatigupta during the reign of Pravarasena II; found near Poona in 1912 |\n\nThe Miregaon inscriptions are an important source for Vakataka history: Prabhavati Gupta is learnt to have been the **wife of Rudrasena II** and the **daughter of Gupta ruler Chandra Gupta II**. In these inscriptions, she describes herself as the mother of Vakataka Maharajasri Damodarasena and Pravarasena. These inscriptions serve as the main source for determining Vakataka chronology on the basis of contemporaneity between the Gupta and Vakataka rulers.\n\n**Vatsagulma Branch Inscriptions**\n\nThe Basim inscriptions of Vakataka King Vindhyasakti II, issued in his 37th regnal year and found in 1939 in the Akola District of Vidarbha, were the first to throw light on the Vatsagulma branch. Before this discovery, historians held the opinion that there was only one Vakataka branch. According to Prof. V.V. Mirasi, after the death of Pravarasena I, the Vakatakas divided into four branches — Gouthamiputra established the Nandivardhana–Pravarapura branch, and Sarvasena started the Vatsagulma branch. Mirasi has stated that nothing is known about the other two branches.\n\n**Hisse–Borala Stone Inscription of Devasena**: Found near Basim in the Akola District. It records that Arya Svamilladeva, an official of Devasena, constructed the Sudarsana lake. Devasena, in his fifth regnal year, also issued Bidar inscriptions granting a village near Bidar.\n\n**Ramtek Inscription of Prabhavati Gupta**: Praises the chivalry of Prabhavati Gupta; issued by her son and her daughter, whose name is not known.\n\n### Coins\n\n*   Contemporary neighbouring dynasties — Kadambas, Vishnukundus, and Ikshvakus — issued their own coins, but the Vakatakas appear not to have issued their own coinage; very few of their coins have been found.\n*   It is assumed the Vakatakas allowed the use of coins minted by other contemporary ruling dynasties. Given their friendly relations with the Gupta kings and Vishnukundus, it is thought they may have used Gupta or Vishnukundu coinage.\n*   Saka Kshatrapa silver coins have been found in the Vakataka Kingdom, both separately and in heaps — from which scholars infer that the Vakatakas permitted the use of Saka Kshatrapa coins in their kingdom.\n*   Silver punch-marked coins (Karshapanas) remained in circulation in India until the medieval period. Since Satavahana and Kshatrapa coins are found in Vakataka territory, P.S. Agrawala opined these coins remained in circulation through the Vakataka period.\n*   Fahien, the Chinese Buddhist traveler (400–414 CE) who visited India during Gupta rule, recorded that people used **cowries** as a medium of exchange.\n*   Some coins of the Nandivardhana–Pravarapura rulers, minted from cheap metals, have been found recently at Paunar near Wardha. On copper coins of Prithvisena II, \"Sri Maharaja Prithvi\" is inscribed in Brahmi script; on coins of a ruler regarded as Narendrasena, \"Sri Rendra\" is inscribed in Brahmi script.\n\n### Material Remains\n\nPhysical remains of Vakataka times have been found at Paunar, Mansar, Nandapur, Ramtek, and Nagara.\n\n*   Excavations carried out in 1967 at Paunar (Pravarapuram) by the Ancient Indian History and Archaeology Department of Nagpur University brought many historical aspects to light, including the material culture of the period.\n*   Habitations existed at Paunar as early as 1000 BCE, before the Vakatakas; by the time of the Vakatakas, Paunar had developed into a political, trade, and cultural center. Coins ranging from ancient punch-marked types to those of the modern Bonsles were found in these excavations.\n*   Excavated buildings followed well-laid plans; finds include bricks of house-tops, round wells, black-and-red earthenware pottery, various implements, household articles, jewellery, and a thread worn round the neck to ward off evil spirits.\n*   Sculptures found include a Durga statue (Mandthal), a statue of Kevala Narasimha in a sitting posture, Vishnu lying on Sesha, and a Buddha image — indicating the dress style, hair decoration, and social and religious beliefs of the period.\n*   The paintings of Buddha and Bodhisattvas in **Caves XVI and XVII at Ajanta**, renowned across the country, stand as symbols of the arts of the period; these were carved under the patronage of Vakataka kings and their feudatories.\n\n### Literary Sources\n\n*   Though the **Puranas** are religion-oriented works, they provide information about ancient India''s royal dynasties and reign periods. Genealogies of royal dynasties appear in the **Vayu, Matsya, Vishnu, Brahamanda, and Bhagavata Puranas**, part of the 18 Puranas — this information is said to be based on the **Bhavishya Purana**. The Puranic account of these dynasties draws to a close with the emergence of the Gupta dynasty.\n*   The Puranas state that the royal dynasty **''Vindhyakula''** was established by Vindhyasakti, and that this dynasty was impure Yavanas. The Puranas also mention that Pravara, son of Vindhyasakti, extended the empire and performed four Asvamedha sacrifices.\n*   **Vistrutha Charitra**, part of Dandi''s _Dasakumara Charitra_, describes in detail how the downfall of the Vakataka Kingdom took place — narrating the conspiracies hatched by the feudatories after Harisena, and the weaknesses of the Vakataka kings. Though written a century after the decline of the Vakataka Kingdom, it remains a valuable account.\n*   The writings of **Kalidasa**, who lived during the time of Chandragupta II, focus on the social, economic, and cultural conditions of the period. Ramagiri, mentioned in Kalidasa''s _Meghadutam_, was identified as ''Ramtek'' by Prof. Mirasi.\n*   The poetical work **''Setubandham''**, written by Vakataka ruler Pravarasena II, also reflects the events of those times.", "free": true}, {"partNumber": 3, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p3", "partName": "Original Home of the Vakatakas", "bodyMd": "Scholars are not unanimous about the original home of the Vakataka royal family, and definite sources locating it beyond doubt are not available. Two main opinions exist — one placing it in north India, the other in south India.\n\n### North India Argument\n\n*   **K.P. Jayaswal**, following Puranic references, located the origin of the Vakatakas in the Bundelkhand–Bagelkhand region (the Vindhya region), specifically near the Kilakila river close to Panna in Bundelkhand. He derived the name \"Vakataka\" from a village called ''Vakat'' or ''Bagat'' in the Jhansi District. He further linked this to the identification of Rudradeva, mentioned in Samudragupta''s Allahabad Pillar Inscription, with Rudrasena I of the Vakataka dynasty.\n*   The Puranas regard the Vakatakas as ''Vindyakas'', with their first ruler named as Vindhyasakti, whose capital was ''Purika'' or ''Chanaka''; Pravara, son of Vindhyasakti, is said to have ruled for 60 years. Identifying Pravara with Pravarasena and Purika with ''Rikshavath'' near the Vindhya mountains, **V.A. Smith** opined that Central India (the Vindhya region) was the original home of the Vakatakas.\n*   In the Nimad District of Madhya Pradesh, four villages carry the name ''Vakad''; since ''Vakatakanam'' in Vakataka inscriptions meant the Vakataka region, **S.V. Sohani** opined that Madhya Pradesh was the first home of the Vakatakas.\n*   Basing his argument on the Puranas, **D.C. Sircar** tried to prove that Eastern Malwa (Vidisa) was the original home of the Vakatakas.\n*   None of the Vakataka inscriptions have been found in northern India. The inscription of Rudrasena I was found at Devtek in Chanda District — suggesting the Vakatakas first established their kingdom in the Vidarbha region, south of the Vindhya mountains. **H.C. Roychoudhary** has stated that the Berar region was the first habitation of the Vakatakas.\n\n### South India Argument\n\n*   **Prof. V.V. Mirasi** opined that South India was the original home of the Vakatakas. He cited an Amaravati inscription of the third century CE, which records a Gahapati (Gruhapati) named ''Vakataka'' who, with his two wives, relatives, and friends, visited the Anala Stupa and made donations for their health and welfare. Since \"Vakataka\" means \"habitant of Vakataka village\", Mirasi felt the village ''Vakata'' might be located in the Amaravati region, and that this Vakataka Gahapati may have been the founder of the Vakataka dynasty. \n*   Other historians counter that, since Amaravati — like Sanchi and Barhut — was a famous Buddhist centre, pilgrims may have come from places like Rajagriha and Pataliputra to visit it, so there is no firm basis for claiming the Vakataka Gahapati actually originated from near Amaravati.\n*   Basing his argument on the similarity of technical terms in the **Basim copper plate inscriptions** of Vakataka King Vindhyasakti II and the Hirahadagalli and **Mydavolu inscriptions** of Pallava Sivaskanda Varma, Mirasi held that the Pallava and Vakataka kingdoms were neighbouring kingdoms in South India. The Basim copper plates refer to Vakataka King Vindhyasakti as ''Haritiputra'' — a title otherwise held only by the Kadambas (Karnataka) and Badami Chalukyas. These inscriptions also praise Vakataka rulers Pravarasena I, Sarvasena, and Vindhyasakti with the title ''**Dharma Maharaja**''.\n*   The **Ghatotkacha Cave Inscription** of Vakataka minister Varahadeva states that ''Vallur'' Brahmins (Hastibhoja, Varahadeva, etc.), belonging to South India, had served as ministers for generations. Mirasi identified this Vallur as the ''Velur'' village in present-day Karimnagar District.\n*   Mirasi held that ''Paunar'', 8 km from Wardha, was the capital of the Vakatakas, built by **Pravarasena II** — a view supported by many historians, since five inscriptions of Pravarasena II have been issued from there.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 4, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p4", "partName": "Chronology", "bodyMd": "There is no unanimity among historians regarding the ruling period of the Vakatakas.\n\n*   Dr. Buhler held that the Vakatakas belonged to the fifth century CE; Dr. Fleet and Kielhorn held that they belonged to the eighth century CE.\n*   The discovery of the Prabhavati Gupta inscriptions near Poona brought historians closer to unanimity on Vakataka chronology. These inscriptions revealed that Prabhavati Gupta was the daughter of Chandragupta II — and on the basis of this contemporaneity between the Vakatakas and the Guptas, it became possible to fix the Vakataka chronology more firmly.\n*   The **copper plate inscriptions** of Vakataka King Vindhyasakti II, found in 1939 at Basim (Vasim) in the Akola District of Vidarbha, added further information. Until this discovery, it was believed that the Vakatakas had only one branch; but based on the **Basim inscriptions**, it was observed that four branches emerged after the death of Pravarasena I.\n*   Overall, it may be said that the Vakatakas ruled from **250 CE to 650 CE**, a period of roughly three centuries.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 5, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p5", "partName": "Caste", "bodyMd": "*   The Puranas make no mention of the caste of the Vakatakas, though they state that the Vindhyakula royal dynasty was established by Vindhyasakti, whose son Pravara performed four Asvamedha sacrifices.\n*   Historians hold the opinion that the ''Vindhyakulu'' and ''Pravarulu'' mentioned in the Puranas, and the Vakatakas mentioned in inscriptions, are one and the same. Others hold that, just as \"Satavahana\" could denote a region or family name, \"Vindhyakulu\" might be the name of a place — since it is common for a family to take its name from its area.\n*   On the basis of the **Ajanta stone inscription**, it has been held that the Vakatakas belonged to the Greek race, and further that — like the Sunga and Kanva rulers — they performed certain rites and obtained brahmanhood, declaring themselves Brahmanas in their inscriptions. It is described in inscriptions that Vindhyasakti was a Brahmin belonging to the ''Vishnu Vruddha'' gotra.\n*   The Vakatakas maintained matrimonial relations with contemporary royal dynas' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || 'ties such as the **Guptas, Nagas, Vishnukundus, and Kadambas.**", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 6, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p6", "partName": "Political Developments — Pravarapura–Nandivardhana Branch", "bodyMd": "### Vindhyasakti I (250 – 270 CE)\n\nVindhyasakti is named in the Puranas as the founder of the Vakataka dynasty, and is also mentioned in the Ajanta inscriptions — the XVI cave inscription describes him as ''**Vakataka Vamsadhvaja**'' and ''**dvija**''.\n\n*   The Puranas describe him as the ruler of the Vidisa region (near Bilsa and Bhopal) and Purika (Vindhya region).\n*   He ruled over the Nagpur and Berar regions as a **feudatory of the Satavahanas** and did not assume any royal titles.\n\n### Pravarasena I (270 – 330 CE)\n\nAfter the death of his father Vindhyasakti, Pravarasena succeeded to the throne. The Puranas state that he ruled for sixty years, and he is regarded as the **real founder of the Vakataka Empire**.\n\n*   His kingdom included northern Maharashtra, Berar, Madhya Pradesh, South Kosala, and Telangana areas.\n*   He occupied Purika territory, earlier ruled by Sisuka.\n*   A follower of Vedic religion, he performed Vedic rites such as Vajapeya, Athiratra, and Agnistoma, along with **four Asvamedha sacrifices**.\n*   In the **Basim inscription of Vindhyasakti II**, he is referred to as ''Samrat'' and ''Dharma Maharaja''; he also declared himself ''**Haritiputra**'' and made large-scale grants to Brahmins.\n\n**Sons of Pravarasena I** According to the Puranas, Pravarasena I had four sons.\n\n*   His eldest son, **Gouthamiputra**, was married to the daughter of Bhavanaga, ruler of the Bharasivanaga dynasty of the Padmavati Kingdom — but Gouthamiputra predeceased his father.\n*   His second son, **Sarvasena**, established the Vatsagulma branch of the Vakataka dynasty.\n*   Neither the names of the other two sons nor the dynasties they may have established are known.\n\n### Pravarapura – Nandivardhana Branch Ruler Table\n\n| Ruler | Reign | Key notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rudrasena I | 330 – 355 CE | Grandson of Pravarasena I (son of Gouthamiputra); capitals at Pravarapura and Nandivardhana |\n| Prithivisena I | 355 – 380 CE | Ruled 25 years; peaceful reign |\n| Rudrasena II | 380 – 385 CE | Adopted Vaishnavism under Prabhavati Gupta''s influence |\n| Divakarasena | 385 – 400 CE | Minor king; Prabhavati Gupta acted as regent |\n| Damodarasena (Pravarasena II) | 400 – 440 CE | Also called Pravarasena II; built Pravarapura city |\n| Narendrasena | 440 – 460 CE | Married Kadamba princess Ajjitabhattarika |\n| Prithvisena II | 460 – 480 CE | Last king of the main branch |\n\n### Rudrasena I (330 – 355 CE)\n\nAfter Pravarasena I, his grandson (son of Gouthamiputra), Rudrasena I, came to the throne.\n\n*   He ruled with Pravarapura (Paunar) in Wardha District and Nandivardhanam in Nagapur District as his capitals.\n*   He maintained relations with the Guptas. His only stone inscription is found at Chandrapur.\n*   Since Bhavanaga of Bharasivanaga is referred to in Vakataka inscriptions, it is thought Rudrasena I may have received Bharasivanaga support.\n*   Rudrasena faced several political convulsions — the Vakataka Kingdom got divided into four parts due to the opposition of his uncles, and Vakataka authority became blurred for a time. With the help of his maternal grandfather, the ruler of Padmavati Kingdom, he restored the earlier glory of the Vakataka state.\n\n**Historiographical Debate — Rudrasena I and Rudradeva**\n\n1.  **K.P. Jayaswal''s view**: Rudrasena I was the same as Rudradeva, one of the nine rulers of Aryavarta defeated by Samudragupta.\n2.  **A.S. Altekar''s counter-view**: Rudrasena and Rudradeva were not one and the same, but different rulers.\n3.  **Mirasi''s view**: There is no evidence that the Vakatakas remained subordinate to Samudragupta — the Vakatakas did not use the Gupta era in their inscriptions, but their own regnal years. There is also no reference to Vakataka rulers in Samudragupta''s Allahabad Pillar Inscription, suggesting Samudragupta had no enmity with the Vakatakas and instead maintained friendly relations with them — with this strength he may have defeated the Western Kshatrapas.\n\nRudrasena I was a devotee of **Mahabhairava**. During his last days, Vindhyasena, the Basim (Vatsagulma branch) ruler, conquered the Kuntala Kingdom and Bagelakhand.\n\n### Prithivisena I (355 – 380 CE)\n\nRudrasena I was succeeded by his son Prithivisena I, who ruled for 25 years. He was truthful, charitable, knowledgeable, and versatile, and his reign remained peaceful.\n\n*   During his reign, Chandragupta II, the Gupta ruler, defeated the Saka Kshatrapas of Malwa with the help of Rudrasena II.\n*   To strengthen the bond between the two dynasties, Chandragupta II married his daughter **Prabhavati Gupta** to Rudrasena II.\n*   Crown prince Prithivisena shifted the Vakataka capital to Nandivardhana, near Nagpur.\n\n### Rudrasena II (380 – 385 CE)\n\nPrithivisena I was succeeded by his son Rudrasena II, who died within a short period of four or five years.\n\n*   Unlike his ancestors, he did not embrace Saivism; under the influence of his wife Prabhavati Gupta, he adopted Vaishnavism.\n*   During the time of Chandragupta II, Gupta dominance over the Vakataka Kingdom increased, and the Vakataka Kingdom became almost like a feudatory state of the Guptas.\n\n### Divakarasena and Damodarasena (385 – 440 CE)\n\nRudrasena II had two sons — **Divakarasena** and **Damodarasena**.\n\n*   Prabhavati Gupta kept her minor son Divakarasena on the throne and herself acted as regent.\n*   After Divakarasena''s death, Damodarasena was placed on the throne. In this way, acting as regent for her two sons, Prabhavati Gupta discharged administrative responsibilities for **13 years**.\n*   In the **Poona inscriptions**, Prabhavati Gupta declared herself the mother of Divakarasena and Damodarasena, and also declared herself the daughter of Devagupta — historians hold the opinion that Devagupta was none other than Chandragupta II Vikramaditya.\n\n### Pravarasena II (400 – 440 CE)\n\nDamodarasena himself carried the alternate name **Pravarasena II**.\n\n*   His seventeen copper plate inscriptions are found at places such as Amaravati, Wardha, Bitul, Nagapur, Chipndwara, Bhandara, and Balaghat in the Vidarbha region.\n*   The entire Berar area was included in his kingdom.\n*   He built **Pravarapura** city and made it his capital — identified as Paunar in Wardha District.\n*   He was a Saiva devotee who showed tolerance towards other faiths.\n*   A poet and scholar, he wrote the poetic work **''Setubandha''** in Maharashtra Prakrit.\n*   In his new capital, Pravarapura, he built a notable temple for Sreeramachandra.\n\n### Narendrasena (440 – 460 CE)\n\nPravarasena II was succeeded by his son Narendrasena.\n\n*   He married the Kuntala princess **Ajjitabhattarika**, daughter of the Kadamba king Kakutsa Varma.\n*   The rulers of Kosala and Mekala (the kingdom near the Amarakantthaka mountains), and of Malwa, accepted his sovereign authority.\n*   Narendrasena, with the help of the Kadambas, recovered the lost areas and averted further decline of the Vakataka Kingdom. He also occupied territories of the Nala Kingdom in Bastar (Chhattisgarh).\n\n### Prithvisena II (460 – 480 CE)\n\nNarendrasena was succeeded by his son Prithvisena II, the **last king of the main branch** of the Vakatakas.\n\n*   His **Balaghat inscription** states that he restored Vakataka Lakshmi (fortune) twice.\n*   Harisena of the Vatsagulma branch and Bhavadatta Varma of the Nala dynasty are thought to have carried out these raids.\n*   Madhava Varma of the Vishnukund dynasty from Andhra and Telangana also raided and defeated Prithvisena.\n*   Prithvisena gave his daughter in marriage to Madhava Varma; as Prithvisena had no sons, Madhava Varma also received the Vakataka Kingdom.\n*   **Harisena''s Ajanta inscription** mentions the Asmaka Kingdom among the feudatory states subordinate to him. It is learnt that the Vishnukunds themselves fought on Harisena''s side in overthrowing the main Vakataka dynasty.\n*   Coins of the Vishnukunds have been found on a large scale in South Maharashtra, Nagapur, Vidarbha, and Nasik. Vikramendra Bhattaraka Varma II, son of Madhava Varma, declared himself in his inscriptions to be the son of Vakataka Mahadevi, thus linked to both dynasties, and described himself as a very pious person and great poet.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 7, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p7", "partName": "Vakataka — Vatsagulma Branch", "bodyMd": "| Ruler | Reign | Key notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sarvasena | 330 – 355 CE | Founder of the Vatsagulma branch; author of ''Harivijayam'' |\n| Vindhyasena | 355 – 400 CE | Referred to as Vindhyasakti in Basim inscriptions |\n| Pravarasena II | 400 – 415 CE | Succeeded by an eight-year-old son whose name is unknown |\n| Devasena | 450 – 475 CE | Entrusted administration to minister Hastibhoja |\n| Harisena | 475 – 500 CE | Greatest ruler of the branch |\n\n### Sarvasena (330 – 355 CE)\n\nThe Basim copper plate inscriptions and Ajanta inscriptions, found in 1939, provide information about the Vatsagulma branch.\n\n*   Sarvasena, the younger brother of Pravarasena I, founded this branch, making **Basim (Vatsagulma)** in the Akola District of Vidarbha (Berar) his capital.\n*   He conquered the area between the Sahyadri mountains and the river Godavari.\n*   A great patron of literature and a poet himself, he bore the title ''Dharma Maharaja'' and authored **''Harivijayam''**, a work in Prakrit.\n\n### Vindhyasena (355 – 400 CE)\n\n*   Basim inscriptions refer to him as Vindhyasakti; he issued these inscriptions in his 27th regnal year.\n*   It is learnt that Pravara, his minister, defeated the king of Kuntala in the south.\n\n### Pravarasena II (400 – 415 CE)\n\nAfter Vindhyasena, his son Pravarasena II ascended the throne. After his death, his eight-year-old son came to the throne, but sources giving information about the son''s name or rule are not available.\n\n### Devasena (450 – 475 CE)\n\nThe Ghatotkacha cave inscription of Varahadeva II described Devasena as ''Devaraja''.\n\n*   He spent his time in luxuries but entrusted the administration of his kingdom to his able minister, Hastibhoja.\n*   Swamilladeva, an official of Devasena, built a water source called ''Sudarsana'' near Basim.\n\n### Harisena (475 – 500 CE)\n\nAfter Devasena, his son Harisena became ruler — the **greatest ruler among all those of the Vatsagulma branch**. During his period, the name and fame of the Vakataka Kingdom reached its highest point.\n\n**Conquests**\n\n*   Ghurjara (Gujarat)\n*   Malwa\n*   South Kosala (Chhattisgarh)\n*   Kalinga\n*   Asmaka (Telangana)\n*   Kuntala (Southern Maharashtra)\n\n**Extent of Empire**\n\n*   His empire extended from Malwa in the north to Kuntala in the south, and from the Arabian Sea in the west to the Bay of Bengal in the east.\n*   In India, no other contemporary kingdom had such a huge expanse of territory.\n*   Without annexing the conquered kingdoms, he permitted them to continue independent rule on condition that they pay yearly tribute to him.\n\n**Administration and Legacy**\n\n*   He earned a name not only as a conqueror but also as an administrator.\n*   **Varahadeva**, son of Hastibhoja, was his minister, and got Cave 16 excavated at Ajanta; Varahadeva''s inscription has become the main source for knowledge about the Vatsagulma branch.\n\n**Decline After Harisena**\n\n*   After Harisena, the Vakataka Kingdom became weak. Though one or two of his heirs ruled after him, their names are not known.\n*   The Rushika or Asika (Asmaka) region (Karimnagar, Adilabad, and Nizamabad Districts) was ruled by the Vishnukundus as feudatories of the Vakatakas.\n*   At this time, the Kadambas from Karnataka, the Kalachuri kings from northern Maharashtra, and the Nala dynasty rulers from Bastar made raids and occupied several parts of Vakataka territory.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 8, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p8", "partName": "Administrative System", "bodyMd": "Inscriptions are the main source of information on Vakataka administration. The Vakatakas established a stable and effective administrative system.\n\n*   They regarded themselves as the father of their people and did not consider themselves divine-right monarchs; kings did not become autocratic and generally governed according to the Smritis and Dharmasastras.\n*   Vakataka administration was **centralized**. Since transport facilities were not well developed, some decentralization became necessary.\n\n**Administrative Divisions**\n\n*   The empire was divided into **states or provinces** — inscriptions mention Bhojakata State, Arammi State, and Varuchi State.\n*   Each province was divided into **Vishayas**.\n*   Vishayas were divided into **Aharas** and **Bhogas/Bhuktis** (the precise difference between Ahara and Bhoga/Bhukti is not known).\n*   Each Bhoga contained cities, towns, and villages.\n*   In some areas, Vishayas were divided into **Pattes** and **Margas** — the term ''Marga'' is not found in Satavahana or Gupta inscriptions.\n*   Village administration was carried out by village officials; whenever kings made land grants, they used to assemble and inform the village officials. The term ''Gramakuta'' in inscriptions is understood to mean village elders.\n\n**Ministry and Officials**\n\n*   There are not many references to a Mantri Parishat (council of ministers) in Vakataka inscriptions.\n*   Officials named in inscriptions include Mantri, Sachiva, Sarvadhyaksha, Senapati, and Dandanayaka, along with Bhatulu, Chatrulu, Rajukas, Gramakuta, Kulaputra, and Devarika.\n\n**Revenue**\n\n*   Land tax was the main source of government revenue — generally 1/4th to 1/6th of the crop yield was collected as tax.\n*   The government also earned revenue from direct taxes, export and import duties, and held a monopoly over the manufacture of salt and income from liquor manufacture.\n*   Lands granted to scholars, Brahmins, and temples enjoyed exemption from some taxes.\n*   Employees were also given lands in place of salaries.\n*   When government officials visited villages, it was customary for them to be provided with daily necessities — free and compulsory services were required of villagers.\n*   Income from taxes was spent on army maintenance, wars, state administration, and temple construction.\n\n**Military and Judicial System**\n\n*   The Vakatakas maintained a large army; the Senapati supervised military matters.\n*   Vakataka inscriptions mention three kinds of feudatory states: \n\n*   those who surrendered out of fear of invasion\n*   those who surrendered as a result of invasion\n*   those states that were conquered in war.\n\n*   Feudatories looked after law and order in their area, maintained an army, and supplied it to th' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka';
UPDATE theory_content SET concepts_json = concepts_json || 'e ruler in times of war.\n*   In judicial organization, the king remained the highest authority; judgments were based on the Smritis and customs. At the village level, village officials decided cases.\n*   The term ''Sadanda-Vigraha'' in **Vakataka inscriptions** suggests that fines were imposed for small offences, possibly collected in cash.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 9, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p9", "partName": "Economic Conditions", "bodyMd": "*   Internal and external trade, which had flourished under the Satavahanas, declined by the time of the Vakatakas; Srenis (guilds) and towns suffered. The decline of trade between Rome and India is also reflected in the scarcity of Vakataka coins.\n*   Vakataka rulers encouraged agriculture and provided irrigation facilities — dams, canals, and reservoirs were built.\n*   The Hisse–Borala inscription of Vakataka king Devasena states that his official Swamilladeva built the Sudarsana lake. The Ganj Stone inscription of Vyagharadeva also mentions the construction of a reservoir named ''Viraka''.\n*   Vakataka rulers increasingly gave land grants to scholars, Brahmins, government officials, soldiers, and village mahattaras; for the sake of good rewards for their ancestors, they also gave away cows, elephants, horses, and villages, along with special facilities and exemptions for donated villages.\n*   **Basim inscriptions of Vindhyasakti II** list conditions attached to such grants: king''s servants were prohibited from entering the granted villages, some tax exemptions were given, and recipients of land grants were ordained not to commit offences against the ruler or harm other villages.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 10, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p10", "partName": "Social Conditions", "bodyMd": "*   In Hindu society, besides the four varnas, several mixed castes emerged; during Vakataka times, castes had not taken strong roots, and varnas also started losing their purity.\n*   Marriages were mostly celebrated among persons of the same caste — for example, Rudrasena II, a Brahmin, married Prabhavati Gupta, daughter of Chandragupta II.\n*   The Vakatakas maintained relations with the Kadambas and Vishnukundus. Even though the Vakatakas were Brahmins by origin, they followed the Kshatriya profession and were called **Brahma Kshatriyas**.\n*   In this age, respect for Brahmins increased; they not only became scholars but also earned fame as administrators. The Vakatakas employed Brahmins as ministers — kings of the Vatsagulma branch employed Brahmins such as Hastibhoja and Varahadeva as ministers.\n*   Women like Prabhavati Gupta took up administrative responsibilities.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 11, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p11", "partName": "Religious Conditions", "bodyMd": "Both Vedic religion and Buddhism flourished in the Vakataka kingdom. The majority of Vakataka rulers were **Saivites**, and some were Vaishnavas.\n\n*   **Pravarasena II**, due to the influence of his mother, queen Prabhavati Gupta, converted to Vaishnavism; he was described as a devotee of Chakrapani and as ''Paramabhagavata''. Inscriptions describe Prabhavati Gupta herself as ''**Atyanta Bhagavadbhakta**'' and ''**Paramabhagavata**''.\n*   **Prithvisena II** was also a Vaishnava. Other Vakataka rulers such as **Pravarasena I, Rudrasena I, and Prithivisena I** were Saivas. \n*   Rudrasena I was a devotee of Mahabhairava; Prithivisena I was a devotee of Mahesvara and built a Siva temple known as Dharmasthana, dedicated to his beloved goddess. Pravarasena II was a devotee of Sambhu and built a Ramachandra temple in his capital, Pravarapuram.\n*   Temple construction went on briskly during Vakataka times — at Ramtek (Maharashtra), the Ramachandra temple, Varaha temple, Vamanavatara (Trivikrama) temple, and Kevala Narasimha temple were built.\n*   While the rulers of the main Vakataka branch followed Vedic religion, those belonging to the Vatsagulma branch were followers of **Buddhism**. The rulers of the Anoopa and Rishika regions, feudatories of the Vatsagulma branch, patronized Buddhism.\n*   **Varahadeva**, minister of Harisena, was a supporter of Buddhism and got cave temples cut at Ajanta and Ghatotkacha for the sake of Buddhists.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 12, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p12", "partName": "Educational and Literary Development", "bodyMd": "Sanskrit and Prakrit literatures flourished during the time of the Vakatakas. The Vakatakas issued their inscriptions in Sanskrit, and the rulers themselves not only patronized literature but also wrote poetical works and works on morals.\n\n*   Dandi particularly praised the Sanskrit style of **Vaidarbhi** associated with Vakataka times.\n*   Vakataka king Sarvasena wrote **''Harivijayam''**, a poetical work in Prakrit, along with some Prakrit stories (verses). Because of his efforts, Vatsagulma flourished as an important educational and cultural centre. ''Harivijayam'' — praised by Dandi — narrates the story of Srikrishna obtaining the Parijatam tree from heaven; though the work itself is not available today, later writers referenced events narrated in it.\n*   **''Setubandham''**, a poetical work in Prakrit regarded as written by Pravarasena II, was praised by Banabhat and Ramadas as the most famous poetical work of its time, containing 15 aswasas (chapters). Kshemendra, in his work ''Ayochitya Vichara Charcha'', also praised it as most wonderful. Since Pravarasena mentioned Kalidasa''s name at the end of each aswasa in ''Setubandhu'', some scholars regard it as a combined work of both Pravarasena and Kalidasa.\n*   Some scholars hold that Kalidasa was sent to the Vakataka Kingdom by the Gupta ruler Chandragupta II, and that at Ramagiri, Kalidasa wrote **''Meghasandesam'' (Meghadutam)**; Ramagiri was identified with present-day Ramtek by V.V. Mirasi.\n*   Pravarasena, like his grandfather and mother, was a **paramabhagavata** and a devotee of Rama. His poetical work mentions the episode of Sriramachandra''s raid on Sri Lanka, referencing the mountains along Rama''s route — Vindhya, Sahya, Malaya, and Mahendragiri.\n*   During Vakataka times, Pravarapura (Paunar) and Vatsagulma (Basim) flourished as great educational centres; Brahmadeyas and Agraharas emerged as centres of Vedic study.", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 13, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p13", "partName": "Development of Architecture, Sculpture and Painting", "bodyMd": "The architecture and sculpture of the Vakatakas can be seen in their temples and the Ajanta caves. Structural temples and cave temples were built at places such as Paunar, Mansar, Nandapur, Ramtek, and Nagapur, showing the influence of Gupta architecture.\n\n*   At **Ramtek**, Vakataka rulers built the Ramachandra temple, Varaha temple, Vamanavatara (Trivikrama) temple, and Kevala Narasimha temple.\n*   **Pravarasena II** built the Ramachandra temple at Pravarapura. According to the Ramtek inscription, Rudrasena I built a Saiva temple known as ''Devasthanam'' at Chikkamburi.\n*   Vakataka sculptures are characterized by a long body, a lean and delicate waist, and strong upper parts — features resembling the Satavahana sculptures at Amaravati, Nagarjunakonda, and Jaggayyapeta. Sculptures at Paunar, Mandthal, Ramtek, and Jabalpore stand as symbols of Vakataka sculptural art. Nandivardhanam and Pravarapura flourished as the main art centres.\n*   In the 15th cave at Ajanta, Ganga-Yamuna sculptures were carved; in Cave numbers 16, 17, and 19, danceuse figures were carved in place of Ganga-Yamuna figures — the carving of danceuse figures in Buddhist sculpture began in Vakataka times.\n\n**Ajanta Sculptures and Paintings**\n\n*   Ajanta''s sculptures and paintings have been described by critics as an extraordinary art creation, continuing over approximately seven centuries. There are 30 caves at Ajanta, unexpectedly recognized by **John Smith in 1919**; some caves belonged to the Theravada tradition and others to Mahayana.\n*   Buddhist monks **Buddhabhadra, Madhuradasudu, and Jaranta Dharmadatta** also carved out Buddha statues at Ajanta. Vakataka-period paintings are also found at Aurangabad, Ghatotkacha (Aurangabad District), and Bagh (Madhya Pradesh). Inscriptions issued by the Vakataka kings and their feudatories are found throughout the Ajanta caves.\n\n| Cave No. | Associated With / Excavated By | Key Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cave 1 | — | Mahajanaka Jataka painted; Vihara has a Buddha statue in Dharmachakrapravartana pose, flanked by Padmapani and Vajrapani — the Padmapani (Avalokitesvara) painting is a masterpiece among Ajanta paintings |\n| Cave 16 | Excavated by Varahadeva, minister of Harisena | Considered the leading cave among all; depicts the end episodes in the life of Buddha along with Jataka stories |\n| Cave 17 | Cut by Upendra Gupta of the Rishika Kingdom, a feudatory of Harisena | ''Maha Hamsa Jatakam'' and ''Mathruposhaka Jatakam'' scenes; has a greater number of paintings than other caves, well-preserved without spots or damage |\n| Cave 19 | — | Scene of Buddha wandering as a beggar before Yasodhara and Rahul in Kapilavastu |\n| Cave 26 | — | Deepankara Buddha of the Deepankara Jataka carved several times; the ''Mahaparinirvana'' of Buddha also carved here |", "free": false}, {"partNumber": 14, "conceptId": "telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka-p14", "partName": "Conclusion", "bodyMd": "The Vakatakas were the first among the dynasties to rule the Deccan after the Satavahanas, with Adilabad, Karimnagar, and Nizamabad areas of Telangana among their territories. It is notable that the Vishnukunds, who later ruled independently in these areas, had been feudatories of the Vakatakas. \n\nThrough matrimonial relations with the Guptas in the north and the Kadambas in the south, the Vakatakas built strong foundations for stable rule and the maintenance of law and order across their empire. Prominence was given to people''s welfare and to the maintenance and capability of the army in administrative and economic affairs. In educational and cultural fields, steps were taken for the promotion of Sanskrit and Prakrit and the running of educational centres; Saiva, Vaishnava, and Buddhist religions all received patronage, and mixed castes emerged in society. \n\nSeveral Hindu temples at the capitals of Paunar and Basim, and Buddha''s life stories and Jataka stories, were brought out in highly striking modes of sculpture and painting. Hence, the Vakataka rule period stands memorable in Deccan history.", "free": false}]' WHERE chapter_slug = 'telangana-history-en-chapter3-vakataka';
